/dts-v1/;
/plugin/;

/*
 * light-desk: activate internal pull-up input mode for 7 button GPIO
 * pins on PocketBeagle 1 - the 6 control-desk buttons (P2.02, P2.04,
 * P2.06, P2.20, P2.22, P2.24) plus P2.33, the stand-alone-mode
 * toggle button (see scripts/standalone_mode_toggle.py and
 * memo.md item 15) - read from userspace via libgpiod by
 * scripts/buttons_debug_print.py / scripts/ads7830_to_osc.py /
 * scripts/standalone_mode_toggle.py (see pb1/memo.md item 9 for the
 * original 6-pin selection history).
 *
 * The base am335x-pocketbeagle.dts (see /opt/source/dtb-*.x/src/arm/
 * ti/omap/am335x-pocketbeagle.dts on the board, under &am33xx_pinmux)
 * already defines a ready-made pinctrl-single node per header pin,
 * pre-configured exactly as PIN_INPUT_PULLUP + MUX_MODE7 (GPIO) -
 * P2_02_gpio, P2_04_gpio, P2_06_gpio, P2_20_gpio, P2_22_gpio,
 * P2_24_gpio, P2_33_gpio below - but none of them are referenced by
 * any device's pinctrl-0, so they're never actually applied; the
 * pins sit at whatever mux/bias U-Boot/ROM left them in at boot
 * instead. Confirmed on real hardware: with nothing wired to any of
 * these pins, `gpioget -b pull-up|pull-down|disabled` all returned
 * the exact same fixed value per pin no matter the bias flag - i.e.
 * libgpiod's runtime bias request has zero effect here (this
 * reproduced on unrelated pins too, e.g. P2.02/P2.03, so it's not
 * specific to these - AM335x's pinctrl-single driver doesn't support
 * runtime bias changes through the generic Linux pinctrl API that
 * libgpiod's -b option goes through; the pull config has to be the
 * static pinmux value applied at boot, which is exactly what this
 * overlay does).
 *
 * 6 of the 7 pins (P2.02, P2.04, P2.06, P2.22, P2.24, P2.33) sit on
 * GPIO bank gpio1; P2.20 sits on gpio2 instead. Two small fragments,
 * one per bank, cover this: any platform device with a bound driver
 * gets pinctrl-0 applied automatically at probe time (generic Linux
 * driver-core behavior, independent of what the driver itself
 * does), and both gpio1's and gpio2's own omap-gpio driver instances
 * already probe/bind normally - so adding pinctrl-names/pinctrl-0
 * here applies the pad config without any device claiming the GPIO
 * lines themselves (unlike e.g. a gpio-keys or gpio-leds node, which
 * would also reserve the lines and block libgpiod from requesting
 * them from userspace - not what we want here, since the existing
 * Python scripts poll these lines directly).
 */

&gpio1 {
	pinctrl-names = "default";
	pinctrl-0 = <&P2_02_gpio &P2_04_gpio &P2_06_gpio &P2_22_gpio &P2_24_gpio &P2_33_gpio>;
};

&gpio2 {
	pinctrl-names = "default";
	pinctrl-0 = <&P2_20_gpio>;
};
